            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus identifies multiple uses of 'hate' in Scripture, including literal emotional malice or hostility (especially in moral contexts like 1 John), a Semitic idiom of comparative preference meaning to 'love less,' and covenantal rejection or non-election (e.g., God hating Esau).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines 'hate' strictly as the action of 'un-choosing' or repudiating a covenant partner or claim in favor of another, enacted through refusal or sending away rather than being an emotion or a mere softening to 'love less.'\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Emotional hostility, comparative preference ('loving less'), and covenantal rejection/repudiation.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"To un-choose or repudiate a covenant partner or claim in favor of another.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book rejects the 'love less' comparative paraphrase and the view that hate is primarily an emotion, restricting the definition exclusively to the decisive action of covenantal repudiation or un-choosing.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book claims 'hate' enacts repudiation and is not merely 'love less,' whereas the consensus frequently defines the biblical idiom (especially in Luke 14 and Genesis 29) precisely as 'loving less.'\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book adopts one recognized strand of the consensus—covenantal rejection and repudiation of allegiance—but elevates it to the sole definition, explicitly rejecting the consensus's inclusion of 'love less' and 'emotion' as primary meanings.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"In moral texts like 1 John, hatred of a brother is equated with spiritual murder, which strongly implies emotional malice rather than just covenantal un-choosing. Additionally, Genesis 29:30 explicitly uses 'loved more' immediately before stating Leah was 'hated,' lending heavy support to the comparative 'loved less' reading the book criticizes.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
